          <h2>Why nature, and why locally?</h2>
          <p>Relief is not only about parcels and shelter overseas. In the UK too, many people are lonely, under strain or shut out of the places and activities that help others feel well. Time outdoors, in company and without pressure, is one of the simplest and lowest-cost ways to help.</p>
          <p>This is the local side of what RallyGood does. International relief is delivered through <a class="text-link" href="drops.html">Drops</a>. Nature and wellbeing programmes are an ongoing part of how the charity works closer to home, in the UK, and they are not run as time-limited appeals.</p>

          <h2 id="forest-bathing">What is forest bathing?</h2>
          <p>Forest bathing is the practice of spending slow, unhurried time among trees, using all of your senses. It began in Japan in the 1980s, where it is known as <em>shinrin-yoku</em>, which can be translated as "taking in the forest atmosphere".</p>
          <p>It is not a hike, a fitness class or a nature lecture. There is no destination and no target pace. A trained facilitator invites people to slow right down, notice what is around them and take part only as far as feels comfortable.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sight:</strong> light through leaves, shapes, colours, movement.</li>
            <li><strong>Sound:</strong> birdsong, wind, water, and quiet.</li>
            <li><strong>Touch and smell:</strong> bark, moss, soil, air after rain.</li>
            <li><strong>Pace:</strong> slow walking, pausing, sitting, and sharing only if you wish.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Why we think it matters</h2>
          <p>Research on spending time in green space and woodland, including forest bathing studies, suggests it can be associated with lower stress and better mood for many people. The evidence is still developing and results vary, so we do not claim that forest bathing treats or cures any condition.</p>
          <p>What we can say is simpler: for people who are isolated or under pressure, a calm, free, welcoming session outdoors can be a way to rest, meet others and feel part of something. It is an offer of wellbeing and inclusion, not medical care.</p>

          <h2>Who the programmes are for</h2>
          <p>Our purposes are to support community wellbeing and social inclusion through access to nature-based and other programmes for people experiencing disadvantage, isolation or barriers to participation. In practice we expect to welcome people who might otherwise find it hard to take part, for example because of cost, confidence, loneliness, or not knowing where to start. Everyone is welcome to join.</p>
