        <div class="prose">
          <h2>What a session may look like</h2>
          <p>Each session will be shaped by its facilitator, the site and the group. As a guide, a session might include:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>A welcome and a short explanation of what to expect, with no pressure to take part in anything you don't want to.</li>
            <li>A gentle, slow walk with pauses to notice what you see, hear, smell and feel.</li>
            <li>Time to sit quietly on your own, or somewhere comfortable to rest.</li>
            <li>An optional, relaxed chance to share and a hot drink, if the venue allows.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Sessions are planned to be short enough to be manageable, at an easy pace. You can pause, sit out or leave at any time.</p>

          <h2>Accessibility and inclusion</h2>
          <p>We want these sessions to be open to people with a range of needs and abilities. Before each session we will aim to say what the site is like, how far people will walk, and whether paths are step-free, and we will ask people to tell us about any adjustments that would help. Nobody should be turned away because they cannot give, and we will always welcome a conversation about what would make attending possible.</p>

          <h2>Safety and safeguarding</h2>
          <p>Outdoor sessions carry risks, so we plan to carry out a risk assessment for each site and to make sure facilitators are trained and appropriate checks are in place. Our <a class="text-link" href="safeguarding.html">safeguarding summary</a> sets out how we intend to protect children and adults at risk, including photo consent. Forest bathing is not a substitute for medical or mental health care. If you are unwell or worried about your health, please speak to your GP or a qualified professional first.</p>

          <h2>How the programmes are funded</h2>
          <p>International Drops are time-limited appeals with a target and close-out report. Local nature and wellbeing work is different: it is an ongoing part of what RallyGood does, supported by general giving, the optional session donation and, in time, grants and sponsorship. We are not yet accepting donations, and we will explain clearly how any gift is used before anyone gives.</p>
        </div>
